[[File:Devil's Staircase at Lake Wakatipu aerial panorama.jpg|thumb|Devil's Staircase at Lake Wakatipu aerial panorama]]
'''Lake Wakatipu''' ({{langx|mi|Whakatipu Waimāori}}) is an inland [[lake]] ([[finger lake]]) in the [[South Island]] of New Zealand. It is in the southwest corner of the [[Otago]] region, near its boundary with [[Southland, New Zealand|Southland]]. The name ''Wakatipu'' comes from the [[Māori language|Māori]] name of the lake; there are two different etymologies as to the origin of the name.

With a length of {{convert|80|km|mi}}, it is New Zealand's longest lake, and, at {{convert|295|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}}, its [[List of lakes in New Zealand#Largest lakes|third largest]]. The lake is also very deep, its floor being below sea level (−111 metres, or 364 feet), with a maximum depth of {{convert|420|m|ft}}. It is at an altitude of {{convert|309|m|ft|abbr=on}}, towards the southern end of the [[Southern Alps]]. The general topography is a reversed "N" shape or "dog leg". The [[Dart River / Te Awa Whakatipu|Dart River]] flows into the northern end, the lake then runs south for 30 kilometres before turning abruptly to the east. {{convert|20|km|mi|abbr=off|spell=In}} further along, it turns sharply to the south, reaching its southern end {{convert|30|km|mi}} further south, near [[Kingston, New Zealand|Kingston]]. At the north end of the lake is the settlement of [[Glenorchy, New Zealand|Glenorchy]], in the north-east corner, and the smaller isolated locality of [[Kinloch, Otago|Kinloch]] in the north-west corner.

The lake is drained by the [[Kawarau River]], which flows out from the lake's only arm, the Frankton Arm, {{convert|8|km|mi|abbr=on}} east of [[Queenstown, New Zealand|Queenstown]]. Until about 18,000 years ago the [[Mataura River]] drained Lake Wakatipu. The [[Kingston Flyer]] follows part of the former river bed now blocked by glacial moraine.<ref>{{citation| url=http://www.doc.govt.nz/documents/science-and-technical/casn276.pdf |title=Geology of the southeastern Eyre Mountains relevant to tenure review |last=Turnbull |first=I M|year=2000 |publisher=Department of Conservation |location=Wellington, N.Z. |issn = 1171-9834}}</ref> Queenstown is on the northern shore of the lake close to the eastern end of its middle section. It has a [[seiche]] period of 26.7 minutes which, in Queenstown Bay, causes the water level to rise and fall some {{convert|200|mm|in|0}}.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.orc.govt.nz/managing-our-environment/water/water-monitoring-and-alerts/kawarau/lake-wakatipu-at-willow-place|title=Lake Wakatipu at Willow Place|website=ORC|language=en|access-date=31 May 2019}}</ref><!--<ref>[http://answers.google.com/answers/threadview?id=703712 Google Answers:Why does Lake Wakatipu in New Zealand rise and fall?]</ref>-->

Lake Wakatipu is known for its scenery and is surrounded by mountains. Two mountain ranges, [[the Remarkables]] and the [[Tapuae-o-Uenuku / Hector Mountains]], lie along its southeastern edge. It is a common venue for [[Adventure travel|adventure tourism]], with [[skiing|skifields]], [[paragliding]], [[bungy jumping]] and [[Tramping in New Zealand|tramping tracks]] within easy reach. A vintage steamboat, the [[TSS Earnslaw|TSS ''Earnslaw'']] regularly plies its waters. Several [[vineyard]]s are nearby in [[Gibbston]].

