==Etymology==
The name ''Wakatipu'' is either a contraction of {{lang|mi|Waka-tipua-wai-maori}} or derived from {{lang|mi|Whakatipu}}.<ref name="linz">{{cite LINZ|26292|26292}}</ref>

{{lang|mi|Waka-tipua-wai-maori}} means 'trough of fresh water where the giant lies' and comes from a legend that a giant created the lake when he died with the giant's inhalations and exhalations explaining the changing water levels. {{lang|mi|Whakatipu}} means 'to create' or 'to cause to grow'. The name is believed to come from a story about how the remainder of a Māori tribe sought refuge around the lake to rebuild their numbers.<ref name="linz"/>

Wakatipu could mean "growing bay" if the original was Whakatipu and the h elided as a result of the [[Māori language#South Island dialects|Southern Māori dialect]].<ref name=RSNZ /> The dialect is also known for dropping final vowels. ''Waka'' can also mean 'hollow'.<ref name=RSNZ>
{{cite journal
  |url=http://rsnz.natlib.govt.nz/volume/rsnz_52/rsnz_52_00_000700.html
  |title=Art. XII.—The Southern Maori, and Greenstone: Addenda
  |journal=Transactions and Proceedings of the Royal Society of New Zealand
  |volume=52|year=1920
  |author=H. Beattie
  |pages=51–52}}
</ref>

Another proposed etymology is that the name is derived from the name of a [[Māori canoe]] (called a ''waka'' in the Māori language).<ref name="goldendays"/>

==History==
Lake Wakatipu was first recorded on a map in 1843, it was not actually surveyed at the time and its position was based on details given by Māori; in September 1853 [[Nathaniel Chalmers]] became the first European to view the lake being guided to the lake by Reko, a chief of [[Tuturau]]. Chalmers had only viewed the lake from a distance and the first Europeans to reach the lake were a party of three men in January 1856, who were shown a crude map drawn in the ground by Reko. One of the three men accidentally started a large scrub fire when discarding a match beside the lake. More men made the rough trip to see the lake and this led to settlement of the Wakatipu District with the Staircase and Nokomai Stations being established in 1859.<ref name="goldendays">{{cite book | last=Miller | first=Frederick Walter Gascoyne | title=Golden Days of Lake County | publisher=Whitcombe and Tombs | publication-place=Christchurch | date=1973 | isbn=978-0-7233-0194-3|edition=5th|pages=3–9, 11, 13–19, 34–46 }}</ref>

[[William Gilbert Rees]] and [[Nicholas von Tunzelmann]] visited the lake in 1860 and were later granted run holdings surrounding the lake. In 1862 gold was found at [[Dunstan, New Zealand|Dunstan]] leading to the start of the [[Otago gold rush]]. Prospectors came to the lake in search of gold and an influx of people followed after news of gold being found by the lake had spread.<ref name="goldendays"/>

Soon gold was discovered on Rees' land, which lead to the creation of [[Queenstown, New Zealand|Queenstown]].<ref name="goldendays"/>{{RP|56–69}}

== Flooding ==
Lake Wakatipu has experienced periodic flooding<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.orc.govt.nz/media/2955/queenstown-2014-updated-web.pdf|title=Lake Wakatipu flood hazard (ORC)}}</ref> affecting the lakeside communities of Kingston, [[Glenorchy, New Zealand|Glenorchy]] and Queenstown. Notable flooding events include the 1878 Queenstown floods, which affected a large part of the outlying Queenstown and Otago areas, the 1995 Queenstown floods, and most notably the 1999 Queenstown floods, which significantly damaged the Queenstown [[Central business district|CBD]] and road infrastructure resulting in approximately $50 million worth of damage.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.civildefence.govt.nz/resources/photo-library/flood-events-1995-2004/|title=Flood events 1995–2004|website=www.civildefence.govt.nz|language=en|access-date=31 May 2019|archive-date=28 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210128143921/https://www.civildefence.govt.nz/resources/photo-library/flood-events-1995-2004/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.odt.co.nz/regions/queenstown-lakes/learning-big-flood|title=Learning from the big flood|last=Williams|first=Guy|date=15 November 2014|website=[[Otago Daily Times]] |language=en|access-date=31 May 2019}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.tvnz.co.nz/one-news/new-zealand/back-in-the-day-queenstown-hit-by-extreme-flooding-6133099|title=Back in the Day: Queenstown hit by extreme flooding|work=[[TVNZ]]|access-date=31 May 2019|language=en-NZ}}</ref>[[File:Lake Wakatipu-HD.jpg|thumb|Lake Wakatipu from Queenstown Hill]]

==Wildlife==
Lake Wakatipu is a habitat for the [[New Zealand longfin eel|longfin eel]] (a specimen caught in 1886 is the largest known of this species<ref>[http://www.fishing-worldrecords.com/scientificname/Anguilla%20dieffenbachii/show Fishing World Records]</ref>), and for introduced [[brown trout]], [[salmon]] and [[rainbow trout]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.nzfishing.com/FishingWaters/Otago/OTFishingWaters/OTWakatipuLake.htm |title=Lake Wakatipu Fishing |access-date=28 April 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141220001439/http://www.nzfishing.com/FishingWaters/Otago/OTFishingWaters/OTWakatipuLake.htm |archive-date=20 December 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> These and other fish support predators such as the [[Australian pied cormorant|pied shag]]. The [[black-billed gull]] is often found around the lake while the most common birds are the [[black-billed gull]] and the introduced [[mallard]]. A smaller bird often not noticed because of its size is the [[New Zealand scaup]].

